{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_011tvLHSr4sDpJLDfVFn9aFk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01QykNXXKPjWDn35tMacLPRg","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty**, **Related Work**, and **Significance**.\n\n## Paper: \"The Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff: Why CKA-Aware Pruning Helps SFT but Breaks RLVR in Reasoning Models\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper introduces RASP (Recovery-Aware Structural Pruning), a CKA-based scoring function S(h) = I(h)·(1−F(h))^α that preferentially removes functionally redundant structures in reasoning LLMs. The key finding is the \"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\": RASP achieves best SFT recovery (+5.75pp at 25% sparsity, p=0.008) but accelerates GRPO collapse by 6× (step 10 vs step 60). Three claims: (1) GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling is universal, pruning adds training collapse; (2) Collapse severity diagnoses structural damage; (3) Recovery paradigm conditions optimal pruning.\n\n### Related Work Covered in Paper\n1. **Structural pruning for LLMs:** LLM-Pruner [Ma et al., 2023], ShortGPT [Men et al., 2024], GRASPrune [Wang et al., 2026], Bonsai [Li et al., 2025]\n2. **RLVR alignment for reasoning:** GRPO [Shao et al., 2025], DeepSeek-R1 [DeepSeek-AI, 2025], Qwen2.5-Math [Yang et al., 2025], Self-Healing [Zhang et al., 2025]\n3. **CKA for model analysis:** Kornblith et al. [2019], MPruner [Chen et al., 2024]\n\nTotal references: ~10\n\n### Key Methodological Contribution\nRASP scoring: S(h) = I(h)·(1−F(h))^α where:\n- I(h) = standard importance score (magnitude/gradient)\n- F(h) = CKA-based functional redundancy (intra-layer similarity to most similar peer)\n- α controls redundancy penalty strength\n\n### Key Empirical Findings\n1. GRPO never improves accuracy on any model (unpruned: 81.27% before and after GRPO, Δ=0.00pp)\n2. Structural pruning causes GRPO training collapse; unstructured pruning does not\n3. RASP has best SFT recovery but fastest GRPO collapse (Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff)\n4. Collapse speed correlates with redundancy removal across 5 methods\n5. Per-structure regression: F(h) anticorrelates with SFT activation change (r=-0.42) but predicts collapse speed (r=0.55)\n6. Cross-model replication on Qwen2.5-Math-7B with consistent 5-6× collapse ratio\n\n### Models and Scale\n- DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (7.62B params)\n- Qwen2.5-Math-7B\n- Both are 7B scale only\n\n### Self-Healing Comparison\nSelf-Healing [Zhang et al., 2025] claims RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models at 25% compression. This paper reconciles: unstructured pruning (as Self-Healing uses) doesn't collapse, but structural pruning does.\n\n### Discussion and Limitations (from paper)\n- Limited to 7B-scale models\n- GSM8K-centric evaluation\n- SFT gap at 20% sparsity not significant (p=0.25), only significant at 25% (p=0.008)\n- No solution to GRPO collapse proposed\n- GRPO run for only 100 steps\n\n### Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Novelty** — Is the Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff a genuinely new insight? Is RASP's CKA-based scoring novel? Are the claims about GRPO collapse on pruned models new to the literature?\n\n2. **Positioning vs. related work** — Is the paper properly positioned? Are key related works missing? Is the comparison with Self-Healing fair and complete?\n\n3. **Significance** — Would this change how people think about pruning + alignment? Is the finding actionable?\n\n4. **Scope and generalization** — Two 7B models, one primary benchmark (GSM8K). Is this sufficient? What about larger models, other RL algorithms, other tasks?\n\n5. **Conceptual clarity** — Is the \"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\" well-defined and well-supported? Is the narrative coherent?\n\n6. **Missing related work** — Consider: knowledge distillation for compressed models, other RL methods (PPO, DPO), pruning-at-training approaches, lottery ticket hypothesis, etc.\n\n7. **Reference quality** — Only ~10 references. Is this adequate?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing Related Work** (specific papers/areas)\n- **Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on novelty, positioning, and significance, not experimental minutiae."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":36144,"cache_read_input_tokens":22960,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":36144},"output_tokens":18,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4bc2a3bd-d50f-4f56-b18b-27b29ed56804","uuid":"1244d035-cad6-4572-a826-9ebe5d375db4"}
{"type":"system","subtype":"task_started","task_id":"a1b9c4316c01d5277","tool_use_id":"toolu_01QykNXXKPjWDn35tMacLPRg","description":"R2: Novelty + Related Work review","task_type":"local_agent","prompt":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty**, **Related Work**, and **Significance**.\n\n## Paper: \"The Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff: Why CKA-Aware Pruning Helps SFT but Breaks RLVR in Reasoning Models\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper introduces RASP (Recovery-Aware Structural Pruning), a CKA-based scoring function S(h) = I(h)·(1−F(h))^α that preferentially removes functionally redundant structures in reasoning LLMs. The key finding is the \"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\": RASP achieves best SFT recovery (+5.75pp at 25% sparsity, p=0.008) but accelerates GRPO collapse by 6× (step 10 vs step 60). Three claims: (1) GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling is universal, pruning adds training collapse; (2) Collapse severity diagnoses structural damage; (3) Recovery paradigm conditions optimal pruning.\n\n### Related Work Covered in Paper\n1. **Structural pruning for LLMs:** LLM-Pruner [Ma et al., 2023], ShortGPT [Men et al., 2024], GRASPrune [Wang et al., 2026], Bonsai [Li et al., 2025]\n2. **RLVR alignment for reasoning:** GRPO [Shao et al., 2025], DeepSeek-R1 [DeepSeek-AI, 2025], Qwen2.5-Math [Yang et al., 2025], Self-Healing [Zhang et al., 2025]\n3. **CKA for model analysis:** Kornblith et al. [2019], MPruner [Chen et al., 2024]\n\nTotal references: ~10\n\n### Key Methodological Contribution\nRASP scoring: S(h) = I(h)·(1−F(h))^α where:\n- I(h) = standard importance score (magnitude/gradient)\n- F(h) = CKA-based functional redundancy (intra-layer similarity to most similar peer)\n- α controls redundancy penalty strength\n\n### Key Empirical Findings\n1. GRPO never improves accuracy on any model (unpruned: 81.27% before and after GRPO, Δ=0.00pp)\n2. Structural pruning causes GRPO training collapse; unstructured pruning does not\n3. RASP has best SFT recovery but fastest GRPO collapse (Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff)\n4. Collapse speed correlates with redundancy removal across 5 methods\n5. Per-structure regression: F(h) anticorrelates with SFT activation change (r=-0.42) but predicts collapse speed (r=0.55)\n6. Cross-model replication on Qwen2.5-Math-7B with consistent 5-6× collapse ratio\n\n### Models and Scale\n- DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (7.62B params)\n- Qwen2.5-Math-7B\n- Both are 7B scale only\n\n### Self-Healing Comparison\nSelf-Healing [Zhang et al., 2025] claims RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models at 25% compression. This paper reconciles: unstructured pruning (as Self-Healing uses) doesn't collapse, but structural pruning does.\n\n### Discussion and Limitations (from paper)\n- Limited to 7B-scale models\n- GSM8K-centric evaluation\n- SFT gap at 20% sparsity not significant (p=0.25), only significant at 25% (p=0.008)\n- No solution to GRPO collapse proposed\n- GRPO run for only 100 steps\n\n### Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Novelty** — Is the Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff a genuinely new insight? Is RASP's CKA-based scoring novel? Are the claims about GRPO collapse on pruned models new to the literature?\n\n2. **Positioning vs. related work** — Is the paper properly positioned? Are key related works missing? Is the comparison with Self-Healing fair and complete?\n\n3. **Significance** — Would this change how people think about pruning + alignment? Is the finding actionable?\n\n4. **Scope and generalization** — Two 7B models, one primary benchmark (GSM8K). Is this sufficient? What about larger models, other RL algorithms, other tasks?\n\n5. **Conceptual clarity** — Is the \"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\" well-defined and well-supported? Is the narrative coherent?\n\n6. **Missing related work** — Consider: knowledge distillation for compressed models, other RL methods (PPO, DPO), pruning-at-training approaches, lottery ticket hypothesis, etc.\n\n7. **Reference quality** — Only ~10 references. Is this adequate?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing Related Work** (specific papers/areas)\n- **Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on novelty, positioning, and significance, not experimental minutiae.","uuid":"f3bba9d1-0e5c-4b1f-94ae-a1b7fc8de60c","session_id":"4bc2a3bd-d50f-4f56-b18b-27b29ed56804"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"You are Reviewer R2 for a top ML venue review (acceptance rate ~25%, 6-point scale: 1=Strong Reject, 2=Reject, 3=Weak Reject, 4=Weak Accept, 5=Accept, 6=Strong Accept).\n\nYour focus areas: **Novelty**, **Related Work**, and **Significance**.\n\n## Paper: \"The Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff: Why CKA-Aware Pruning Helps SFT but Breaks RLVR in Reasoning Models\"\n\n### Paper Summary\nThe paper introduces RASP (Recovery-Aware Structural Pruning), a CKA-based scoring function S(h) = I(h)·(1−F(h))^α that preferentially removes functionally redundant structures in reasoning LLMs. The key finding is the \"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\": RASP achieves best SFT recovery (+5.75pp at 25% sparsity, p=0.008) but accelerates GRPO collapse by 6× (step 10 vs step 60). Three claims: (1) GRPO reward-accuracy decoupling is universal, pruning adds training collapse; (2) Collapse severity diagnoses structural damage; (3) Recovery paradigm conditions optimal pruning.\n\n### Related Work Covered in Paper\n1. **Structural pruning for LLMs:** LLM-Pruner [Ma et al., 2023], ShortGPT [Men et al., 2024], GRASPrune [Wang et al., 2026], Bonsai [Li et al., 2025]\n2. **RLVR alignment for reasoning:** GRPO [Shao et al., 2025], DeepSeek-R1 [DeepSeek-AI, 2025], Qwen2.5-Math [Yang et al., 2025], Self-Healing [Zhang et al., 2025]\n3. **CKA for model analysis:** Kornblith et al. [2019], MPruner [Chen et al., 2024]\n\nTotal references: ~10\n\n### Key Methodological Contribution\nRASP scoring: S(h) = I(h)·(1−F(h))^α where:\n- I(h) = standard importance score (magnitude/gradient)\n- F(h) = CKA-based functional redundancy (intra-layer similarity to most similar peer)\n- α controls redundancy penalty strength\n\n### Key Empirical Findings\n1. GRPO never improves accuracy on any model (unpruned: 81.27% before and after GRPO, Δ=0.00pp)\n2. Structural pruning causes GRPO training collapse; unstructured pruning does not\n3. RASP has best SFT recovery but fastest GRPO collapse (Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff)\n4. Collapse speed correlates with redundancy removal across 5 methods\n5. Per-structure regression: F(h) anticorrelates with SFT activation change (r=-0.42) but predicts collapse speed (r=0.55)\n6. Cross-model replication on Qwen2.5-Math-7B with consistent 5-6× collapse ratio\n\n### Models and Scale\n- DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B (7.62B params)\n- Qwen2.5-Math-7B\n- Both are 7B scale only\n\n### Self-Healing Comparison\nSelf-Healing [Zhang et al., 2025] claims RLVR recovers pruned reasoning models at 25% compression. This paper reconciles: unstructured pruning (as Self-Healing uses) doesn't collapse, but structural pruning does.\n\n### Discussion and Limitations (from paper)\n- Limited to 7B-scale models\n- GSM8K-centric evaluation\n- SFT gap at 20% sparsity not significant (p=0.25), only significant at 25% (p=0.008)\n- No solution to GRPO collapse proposed\n- GRPO run for only 100 steps\n\n### Your Task\nEvaluate the paper on:\n\n1. **Novelty** — Is the Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff a genuinely new insight? Is RASP's CKA-based scoring novel? Are the claims about GRPO collapse on pruned models new to the literature?\n\n2. **Positioning vs. related work** — Is the paper properly positioned? Are key related works missing? Is the comparison with Self-Healing fair and complete?\n\n3. **Significance** — Would this change how people think about pruning + alignment? Is the finding actionable?\n\n4. **Scope and generalization** — Two 7B models, one primary benchmark (GSM8K). Is this sufficient? What about larger models, other RL algorithms, other tasks?\n\n5. **Conceptual clarity** — Is the \"Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff\" well-defined and well-supported? Is the narrative coherent?\n\n6. **Missing related work** — Consider: knowledge distillation for compressed models, other RL methods (PPO, DPO), pruning-at-training approaches, lottery ticket hypothesis, etc.\n\n7. **Reference quality** — Only ~10 references. Is this adequate?\n\n### Output Format\nProvide a structured review with:\n- **Summary** (2-3 sentences)\n- **Strengths** (bulleted)\n- **Weaknesses** (bulleted, with severity: major/minor)\n- **Questions for Authors** (numbered)\n- **Missing Related Work** (specific papers/areas)\n- **Score** (1-6) and **Confidence** (1-5)\n\nBe rigorous but fair. Focus on novelty, positioning, and significance, not experimental minutiae."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"toolu_01QykNXXKPjWDn35tMacLPRg","session_id":"4bc2a3bd-d50f-4f56-b18b-27b29ed56804","uuid":"224a59cd-4e7a-4d2d-8b35-215fdd24740a","timestamp":"2026-05-16T15:30:14.459Z"}
